$DIR/main.p:20:5: this program uses timers (time.Sleep, a time.Timer or Ticker, rt.ParkUntil or rt.Now), so its scheduler must provide a clock and a timer, and sched of type main.noClock has no method Now or ArmTimer (abi.md section 12.9)
    Timers need two things from the scheduler: a clock, its Now method, which
    returns monotonic nanoseconds, and a way to be called back, its ArmTimer
    method, which asks for rt.RunTimers to be called once a deadline has passed.
    This program uses timers (time.Sleep, a time.Timer or Ticker, rt.ParkUntil
    or rt.Now), and the bound scheduler lacks one of them.
    The scheduler contract keeps timers optional, so a kernel that never sleeps
    needs no clock. A program that does sleep must get its clock from the
    kernel, because only the kernel knows which timer hardware it drives.
    Give the scheduler a clock, and run the timers from the timer interrupt:
        func (kernelSched) Now() int64 { return int64(ticks) * 1000000 }

        func (kernelSched) ArmTimer(deadline int64) { next = deadline }

        // in the timer interrupt handler:
        if kernelSched{}.Now() >= next {
            rt.RunTimers()
        }
    (see "go starts a puroutine" in the Plutonium spec, section 4.7)
    (run "pluto explain E2595" for a longer explanation)
